      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Certificate number: R76/1992-GB1-10.04\n- Revision 2\n- Issuing authority: National Measurement Office (UK)\n- Member State: United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\n- Date issued: 12 May 2014\n- Reference No: T1128/0107 (this is a project/reference number)\n- Scheme: This is MAA (\"OIML R76 MAA Rev. 1 01 April 2011\" appears). The MAA certificate format... The scheme field is A|B. MAA certificates are typically scheme B? Actually, OIML-CS has two schemes: A (basic) and B (MAA). The mention of \"OIML – MUTUAL ACCEPTANCE ARRANGEMENT\" logo and \"OIML R76 MAA\" suggests scheme B. Hmm, but the certificate is from 2014 under the MAA arrangement. Yes, MAA = Mutual Acceptance Arrangement = Scheme B.\n- Page total: 4\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- name: National Measurement Office\n- address: Stanton Avenue, Teddington, TW11 0JZ, United Kingdom\n- person_responsible: Paul Dixon – Director, Product Certification\n- phone: +44 (0)20 8943 7272\n- website: www.bis.gov.uk/nmo\n- Signatory: G Stones\n\n**Applicant:**\n- CAS Corporation, 19 Ganap-Ri, Gwangjuk-Myoun, Yangju-Si, Gyeonggi-Do 482-841, Republic of Korea\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- \"The applicant\" (CAS Corporation)\n- Shanghai CAS Electronics Co., Ltd, Maixinroad 448, Xinqiaozhen, Songjiangqu, Shanghai, China\n- CAS Elektronik San. Tic. A.S., Yukari Dudulu, Bostanci Cad. Mevdudi Sokak No: 34, Umraniye-Istanbul / Turkey\n\n**Certified type:**\n- category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" — the schema examples say \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\"... For R76 it's a weighing instrument. Category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\"\n- type_designations: SW Series, and models: ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS-M, SW-1, ER JR, PW-II\n- Also SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR are variants of SW-1.\n- module_designation: not applicable? It's a complete instrument, so \"Not applicable\" or null. Load cell is a component: CAS load cell, model SW.\n- description: \"Class III, mains or battery-operated, self-indicating, non-automatic weighing instruments designated SW Series...\"\n\n**Model family:** Definitely — this certificate covers many models with Max/Min/e per model.\n\nModels and variants:\n\nSingle-interval instruments:\n1. ED: Max 3, 6, 15, 30 kg; Min 20, 40, 100, 200 g; e 1, 2, 5, 10 g\n2. ER PLUS / ER PLUS M: Max 6, 15, 30 kg; Min 40, 100, 200 g; e 2, 5, 10 g\n3. SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR: Max 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15, 20, 30 kg; Min 20, 20, 40, 40, 100, 100, 200, 200 g; e 1, 1, 2, 2, 5, 5, 10, 10 g\n4. ER JR: Max 2, 5, 10; Min 20, 40, 100; e 1, 2, 5 (the third table lost its labels: rows are \"2 5 10 | 2 5 10 2\" — OCR damaged; from dual-interval table we see ER JR Max 3/6, 6/15, 15/30 → single-interval likely 3, 6, 15? Hmm wait.\n\nLet me look at the damaged third table:\n\n```\n<table>\n  <tr><td>Model</td><td colspan=\"3\">ER JR</td><td colspan=\"3\">PW-II</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>2</td><td>5</td><td>10</td><td>2</td><td>5</td><td>10</td><td>2</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>20</td><td>40</td><td>100</td><td>20</td><td>40</td><td>100</td><td>20</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>5</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>5</td><td>1</td></tr>\n</table>\n```\n\nThe header row has \"Model\" + ER JR (colspan 3) + PW-II (colspan 3) = 7 cells. Data rows have 7 cells: first cell \"2\" is probably the label \"Max (kg)\" lost and replaced... Actually there are 7 columns: label + 3 ER JR + 3 PW-II. The data rows show \"2, 5, 10, 2, 5, 10, 2\" — that's 7 values but the first should be the label \"Max (kg)\". So the label cell contains \"2\"? That suggests the OCR shifted: the label cell got the first Max value \"2\", and the last column got cut... Hmm, actually if the label cell contains \"2\", then values are: 5, 10, 2, 5, 10, 2 — only 6 values for 6 data columns. ER JR: Max 5, 10, ? Hmm.\n\nWait — compare to dual-interval version:\n```\n<tr><td>Max (kg)</td><td>3 / 6</td><td>6 / 15</td><td>15 / 30</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>2.5 / 5</td><td>4 / 10</td></tr>\n```\nSo ER JR dual: 3/6, 6/15, 15/30; PW-II dual: 1/2, 2.5/5, 4/10.\n\nFor the single-interval damaged table, likely ER JR: 5, 10, 2...? No wait. Let me think about the shift. If the label cell \"Max (kg)\" was dropped and each row lost its first cell, then the row \"2 5 10 2 5 10 2\" would be label=2?? That doesn't work.\n\nAlternative: the row values are actually [label lost, 5, 10, 20, 40...]. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconstruct: Expected table would be:\n- Row: Model | ER JR (3 cols) | PW-II (3 cols)\n- Row: Max (kg) | 5 | 10 | 20? No...\n\nActually from dual-interval: ER JR max dual ranges 3/6, 6/15, 15/30 → single-interval ER JR would be 6, 15, 30? No wait, dual-interval Max is given as Max₁/Max₂ = 3/6 meaning lower/upper capacity. Single-interval Max would be just one Max.\n\nHmm, from the dual table, Min values: ER JR dual Min: 20, 40, 100; e: 1/2, 2/5, 5/10. Compare the damaged single table row 2: \"20 40 100 20 40 100 20\" and row 3: \"1 2 5 1 2 5 1\". So Min: 20, 40, 100 for ER JR and 20, 40, 100 for PW-II. e: 1, 2, 5 for ER JR and 1, 2, 5 for PW-II.\n\nRow 1 \"Max (kg)\": \"2 5 10 2 5 10 2\" — 7 cells. Label cell \"Max (kg)\" was probably replaced by nothing and we have Max values: 2, 5, 10, 2, 5, 10 and trailing \"2\"?? Hmm, that's 6 values + one stray.\n\nWait — dual-interval PW-II: 1/2, 2.5/5, 4/10. So single-interval PW-II Max might be 2, 5, 10. And single-interval ER JR Max... dual is 3/6, 6/15, 15/30. Single would be... hmm, possibly 5, 10, 20? Or maybe the ER JR single Max is 2, 5, 10?\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read: the damaged table row could be: label cell empty→\"2\" placed in label column. So Max values: 5, 10 for ER JR?? That gives only 2 values for 3 columns.\n\nAlternative interpretation: the table actually has 6 data columns but OCR merged. Row \"2 5 10 2 5 10 2\": if label = \"2\"... no.\n\nLet me consider that the single-interval ER JR table is: Max 5, 10, 20? Hmm, but then Min would be 40, 100, 200...\n\nActually the Min row \"20 40 100 20 40 100 20\": if shifted by one (label missing), values would be 40, 100, 20, 40, 100, 20. ER JR Min: 40, 100, 20?? That's weird ordering.\n\nHmm, perhaps the correct reading is: the label cells (\"Max (kg)\", \"Min (g)\", \"e (g)\") were all lost, and each row contains exactly the 6 data values plus one extra. Row 1: 2, 5, 10, 2, 5, 10, \"2\" — maybe the last \"2\" is part of... no.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the table has 6 data columns: ER JR (3): Max 5, 10, 20?? and PW-II (3): Max 2, 5, 10. Row: [label lost → first data value \"2\" lands in label column], then 5, 10, 20?, ... no, we have \"2 5 10 2 5 10 2\".\n\nHmm, actually maybe: ER JR Max: 2, 5, 10 and PW-II Max: 2, 5, 10, and the trailing \"2\" is an OCR artifact. That reads cleanly: row 1: 2, 5, 10 | 2, 5, 10 with label column erroneously getting... wait no, there are 7 cells: \"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"2\". If label cell got \"Max (kg)\" replaced with \"2\"... Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the header row is the issue: \"Model | ER JR (3) | PW-II (3)\" = 7 columns total. Row 1: \"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"2\" = 7 cells. If cell 1 (label column) = \"2\"... then ER JR: 5, 10, 2; PW-II: 5, 10, 2. Weird.\n\nOr if the label column was dropped by OCR and an extra \"2\" appended: ER JR: 2, 5, 10; PW-II: 2, 5, 10, extra \"2\" artifact.\n\nSimilarly Min row: \"20\",\"40\",\"100\",\"20\",\"40\",\"100\",\"20\" → ER JR Min: 20, 40, 100; PW-II Min: 20, 40, 100, extra \"20\".\n\ne row: \"1\",\"2\",\"5\",\"1\",\"2\",\"5\",\"1\" → ER JR e: 1, 2, 5; PW-II e: 1, 2, 5, extra \"1\".\n\nHmm wait, but there's a pattern here: the first cell of each row equals the last cell: 2...2, 20...20, 1...1. That's suspicious — like a circular shift. If the label cell content (\"Max (kg)\") was replaced by the last column's value due to OCR column confusion... Actually a circular shift: original row: [Max (kg), 2, 5, 10, 2, 5, 10] and OCR moved the label out and appended something.\n\nGiven dual-interval PW-II Max: 1/2, 2.5/5, 4/10 → single-interval PW-II Max: 2, 5, 10 makes sense (single-interval Max = upper Max of dual).\nDual-interval ER JR Max: 3/6, 6/15, 15/30 → single-interval ER JR Max would be 6, 15, 30? But the damaged table suggests 2, 5, 10 or 5, 10...\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe not. Single and dual are different variants. The dual-interval ER JR goes 3/6, 6/15, 15/30 (i.e., Max₁=3, Max₂=6 etc.). Single-interval ER JR might be Max 6, 15, 30? Or 3, 6, 15? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at ED: single-interval Max: 3, 6, 15, 30. Dual-interval ED: 1.5/3, 3/6, 6/15, 15/30. So single ED Max values are 3, 6, 15, 30 and dual Max₂ values are 3, 6, 15, 30 (the upper). So single-interval Max = dual upper Max. \n\nER PLUS single: 6, 15, 30; dual: 3/6, 6/15, 15/30. Upper: 6, 15, 30. ✓.\n\nSW-1 single: 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15, 20, 30; dual: 1/2, 1.5/3, 2.5/5, 3/6, 4/10, 6/15, 10/20, 15/30. Upper: 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15, 20, 30 ✓.\n\nPW-II dual: 1/2, 2.5/5, 4/10 → upper: 2, 5, 10. Damaged single table suggests PW-II: 2, 5, 10 ✓.\n\nER JR dual: 3/6, 6/15, 15/30 → upper: 6, 15, 30. So single-interval ER JR Max should be 6, 15, 30? But damaged row shows \"2 5 10 2 5 10 2\"... Hmm, that doesn't contain 6, 15, 30.\n\nHmm wait, maybe I misread. Maybe the ER JR single table is: Max 5, 10, 20? No...\n\nHmm, actually maybe the single-interval ER JR is different: perhaps ER JR single Max: 2, 5, 10? And dual: 3/6, 6/15, 15/30? That's inconsistent with ED pattern but possible — the dual-interval and single-interval variants don't need to have identical capacities... but they usually do (same instrument, dual-range switching).\n\nActually wait, re-reading the damaged table. Maybe the columns are misassigned: maybe \"2 5 10\" belongs to ER JR where actually... Let me check Min consistency. If ER JR single Max: 6, 15, 30 with e: 2, 5, 10 → Min = 20e: 40, 100, 200. But damaged Min row: 20, 40, 100. With Max 2, 5, 10 and e 1, 2, 5 → Min = 20e: 20, 40, 100 ✓ consistent.\n\nFor PW-II single: Max 2, 5, 10, e 1, 2, 5, Min 20, 40, 100 — consistent with damaged table AND with dual upper (2, 5, 10) ✓.\n\nFor ER JR single: dual upper is 6, 15, 30 with dual e upper 2, 5, 10, dual Min 40, 100... wait dual ER JR Min: 20, 40, 100. Hmm.\n\nDual ER JR: Max 3/6, 6/15, 15/30; Min 20, 40, 100; e 1/2, 2/5, 5/10. Min corresponds to Min₁ (min of lower interval): 20×1=20 ✓, 20×2=40 ✓, 20×5=100 ✓.\n\nSo if single ER JR were Max 6, 15, 30, e 2, 5, 10, Min would be 40, 100, 200. But the damaged table shows 20, 40, 100 for both ER JR and PW-II columns...\n\nHmm, actually maybe the damaged table's column assignment is: ER JR: Max 2, 5, 10?? But that contradicts dual 3/6, 6/15, 15/30. Unless... hmm.\n\nOK here's another thought: maybe the damaged single-interval table is actually ER JR: 5, 10, 20 (Max)? With row values \"2 5 10 2 5 10 2\" shifted... I can't make 5,10,20 from \"2 5 10 2 5 10 2\" cleanly.\n\nLet me just go with what the OCR literally shows, interpreting the trailing duplicate as label-column artifact: ER JR single: Max 2, 5, 10; Min 20, 40, 100; e 1, 2, 5. PW-II single: Max 2, 5, 10; Min 20, 40, 100; e 1, 2, 5.\n\nHmm wait, actually — maybe the first cell of each row IS in the label column position but the OCR moved \"Max (kg)\" etc. out. The remaining 7 cells might be: label-placeholder got value from... I think the most sensible reading: the rows have 6 values each and the label cell shows the first value due to a shift where everything shifted left by one and the first value duplicated... no.\n\nActually, you know what — circular: 7 cells: [2, 5, 10, 2, 5, 10, 2]. If the actual data is [Max(kg), 2, 5, 10, 2, 5, 10] and OCR replaced the label with the value that follows in some reading order... whatever. The data: ER JR: 2, 5, 10; PW-II: 2, 5, 10.\n\nHmm, but wait — that conflicts with ER JR dual (3/6, 6/15, 15/30). Single-interval 2, 5, 10 vs dual upper 6, 15, 30. Odd but it's what the document says. Actually hold on, maybe ER JR single really is Max 5, 10, 20? Let me count cells again: `<tr><td>2</td><td>5</td><td>10</td><td>2</td><td>5</td><td>10</td><td>2</td></tr>` — cells: \"2\", \"5\", \"10\", \"2\", \"5\", \"10\", \"2\". 7 cells.\n\nHeader: `<tr><td>Model</td><td colspan=\"3\">ER JR</td><td colspan=\"3\">PW-II</td></tr>` — Model, ER JR (3), PW-II (3) = 7 columns.\n\nSo: label col = \"2\", ER JR = 5, 10, 2; PW-II = 5, 10, 2?? That gives ER JR Max: 5, 10, 2 and PW-II Max: 5, 10, 2. Hmm, that seems wrong ordering.\n\nOr label col got \"Max (kg)\" dropped, and: ER JR = 2, 5, 10; PW-II = 2, 5, 10; last \"2\" overflows into a phantom column.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll go with ER JR single: 2, 5, 10 kg; PW-II single: 2, 5, 10 kg. Hmm, but actually, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe PW-II single Max = 2, 5, 10 (matches dual upper 2, 5, 10 ✓✓✓). And ER JR single Max = 2, 5, 10 doesn't match dual upper 6, 15, 30 ✗. \n\nAlternatively maybe the damaged row is: label=\"Max (kg)\" [lost], ER JR: 5, 10, [20 lost?]... I can't recover. I'll go with the literal reading and note it. Actually, for safety, maybe I should treat ER JR single as Max 2, 5, 10 per the OCR.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider the whole thing. Maybe the last \"2\" isn't an artifact. What if the table has PW-II with... no, colspan says 3.\n\nOK moving on. Actually — one more idea: what if the label column contains \"Max (kg)\" → OCR'd as nothing, and the row is shifted right: label gets \"2\"?? Then values: ER JR: 5, 10, 2 / PW-II: 5, 10, 2. That reads as: ER JR Max: 5, 10, 2 (descending last = weird) and PW-II: 5, 10, 2. Unlikely. The most natural: ER JR: 2, 5, 10; PW-II: 2, 5, 10; trailing 2 = artifact (maybe a repeated column edge).\n\nGiven PW-II = 2, 5, 10 is confirmed by dual table upper bounds, I'm fairly confident. ER JR = 2, 5, 10 is what the OCR literally shows.\n\n**SW-1 variants:** SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR.\n\n**Type designations:** SW Series (family), models: ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M (ER PLUS-M), SW-1 (SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR), ER JR, PW-II.\n\n**Components:**\n- Load cell: CAS load cell, model SW. role: \"load cell\", type_designations: [\"SW\"] (or \"CAS SW\").\n- Mains adaptor: any compatible CE-marked mains adaptor (12 V DC or 9 V DC). Could be a component with alternatives.\n\n**Characteristics:**\n\nType-level:\n- accuracy_class: III\n- temperature_range: -10 to +40 °C\n- power supply: 12 V DC (ED, ER PLUS, SW-1, ER JR) or 9 V DC (PW-II)\n- Battery options:\n  - 6 V 3.6 Ah rechargeable (ED, ER PLUS, ER JR)\n  - 4 × 1.5 V D type (ER JR)\n  - 6 V 3.5 Ah rechargeable (SW-1)\n  - 6 × 1.5 V AA (PW-II)\n- Initial zero setting device: ≤ 20% of Max\n- Semi-automatic zero setting device: ≤ 4% of Max\n- Zero tracking device: ≤ 4% of Max\n- Tare: T ≤ -50% Max (single-interval), T ≤ -Max₁ (dual-interval)\n\nModel-level matrix: Max, Min, e per model per variant (single vs dual interval).\n\nThis is complex: attributes vary per model AND per interval configuration (single vs dual). I'll use model_level with model ids like \"ED (single-interval, Max=3kg)\" or encode model as \"ED 3kg single\" etc. Or use config_level with axis \"interval configuration\" (single-interval vs dual-interval).\n\nLet me structure models. I'll create model entries for each Max variant. That's a lot:\n\nSingle-interval:\n- ED: 3, 6, 15, 30 kg → 4 variants\n- ER PLUS / ER PLUS M: 6, 15, 30 → 3 variants (×2 model names? ER PLUS and ER PLUS M share the table)\n- SW-1 family (SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR): 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15, 20, 30 → 8 variants (×5 sub-models)\n- ER JR: 2, 5, 10 → 3\n- PW-II: 2, 5, 10 → 3\n\nDual-interval:\n- ED: 1.5/3, 3/6, 6/15, 15/30 → 4\n- ER PLUS/M: 3/6, 6/15, 15/30 → 3\n- SW-1 family: 1/2, 1.5/3, 2.5/5, 3/6, 4/10, 6/15, 10/20, 15/30 → 8\n- ER JR: 3/6, 6/15, 15/30 → 3\n- PW-II: 1/2, 2.5/5, 4/10 → 3\n\nTotal distinct model-variant combos is huge (4+3+8+3+3 + same = 42). Encoding every sub-model (5 SW-1 variants × 8) would explode. I'll use model_id like \"ED Max=3kg (single-interval)\" etc., and for SW-1 use \"SW-1S/1C/1W/1LR/1WR Max=2kg (single-interval)\".\n\nAlternatively, use model_level attributes with model ids like \"ED 3kg single\" and \"ED 3kg dual\". Let me define models[] entries with model_id strings:\n\nSingle-interval:\n- \"ED single 3 kg\" (Max 3, Min 20g, e 1g)\n- \"ED single 6 kg\" (Max 6, Min 40g, e 2g)\n- \"ED single 15 kg\" (Max 15, Min 100g, e 5g)\n- \"ED single 30 kg\" (Max 30, Min 200g, e 10g)\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single 6 kg\" (Max 6, Min 40, e 2)\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single 15 kg\" (Min 100, e 5)\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single 30 kg\" (Min 200, e 10)\n- \"SW-1 single 2 kg\" (SW-1S/1C/1W/1LR/1WR; Min 20, e 1)\n- \"SW-1 single 3 kg\" (Min 20, e 1)\n- \"SW-1 single 5 kg\" (Min 40, e 2)\n- \"SW-1 single 6 kg\" (Min 40, e 2)\n- \"SW-1 single 10 kg\" (Min 100, e 5)\n- \"SW-1 single 15 kg\" (Min 100, e 5)\n- \"SW-1 single 20 kg\" (Min 200, e 10)\n- \"SW-1 single 30 kg\" (Min 200, e 10)\n- \"ER JR single 2 kg\" (Min 20, e 1)\n- \"ER JR single 5 kg\" (Min 40, e 2)\n- \"ER JR single 10 kg\" (Min 100, e 5)\n- \"PW-II single 2 kg\" (Min 20, e 1)\n- \"PW-II single 5 kg\" (Min 40, e 2)\n- \"PW-II single 10 kg\" (Min 100, e 5)\n\nDual-interval (Max as \"Max₁ / Max₂\" — I'll capture as maximum_capacity_dual with value string or min/max? Dual Max \"1.5 / 3\" means Max₁=1.5 kg, Max₂=3 kg. I could record as value {min:1.5, max:3}? That's semantically Max₁/Max₂, could map to min/max. Hmm. Safer: value as string \"1.5 / 3\"? The rules say ranges become {min,max}. \"1.5 / 3\" here isn't really a range but two capacity values. I'll record maximum_capacity_max1_max2 as... Actually I'll do: attribute \"maximum_capacity_lower_max1\" and \"maximum_capacity_upper_max2\"? That doubles entries. Or single attribute \"maximum_capacity\" with value {min:1.5, max:3} — using min/max to encode Max₁/Max₂. I think that's acceptable and compact. Similarly e: \"0.5 / 1\" → {min:0.5, max:1}.\n\nDual models:\n- \"ED dual 1.5/3 kg\" (Max 1.5/3, Min 10g, e 0.5/1)\n- \"ED dual 3/6 kg\" (Min 20, e 1/2)\n- \"ED dual 6/15 kg\" (Min 40, e 2/5)\n- \"ED dual 15/30 kg\" (Min 100, e 5/10)\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M dual 3/6 kg\" (Min 20, e 1/2)\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M dual 6/15 kg\" (Min 40, e 2/5)\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M dual 15/30 kg\" (Min 100, e 5/10)\n- \"SW-1 dual 1/2 kg\" (Min 10, e 0.5/1)\n- \"SW-1 dual 1.5/3 kg\" (Min 10, e 0.5/1)\n- \"SW-1 dual 2.5/5 kg\" (Min 20, e 1/2)\n- \"SW-1 dual 3/6 kg\" (Min 20, e 1/2)\n- \"SW-1 dual 4/10 kg\" (Min 40, e 2/5)\n- \"SW-1 dual 6/15 kg\" (Min 40, e 2/5)\n- \"SW-1 dual 10/20 kg\" (Min 100, e 5/10)\n- \"SW-1 dual 15/30 kg\" (Min 100, e 5/10)\n- \"ER JR dual 3/6 kg\" (Min 20, e 1/2)\n- \"ER JR dual 6/15 kg\" (Min 40, e 2/5)\n- \"ER JR dual 15/30 kg\" (Min 100, e 5/10)\n- \"PW-II dual 1/2 kg\" (Min 10, e 0.5/1)\n- \"PW-II dual 2.5/5 kg\" (Min 20, e 1/2)\n- \"PW-II dual 4/10 kg\" (Min 40, e 2/5)\n\nThat's 42 model entries. Attributes: maximum_capacity, minimum_capacity, verification_scale_interval (e). \n\nThe model_level matrix: 3 attributes × 42 models each = big but OK. Actually maybe I'll structure model_level with values arrays. Each attribute entry has 42 values. That's fine.\n\nAlso power supply voltage varies by model: 12 V DC (ED, ER PLUS, SW-1, ER JR) vs 9 V DC (PW-II). That's a model_level attribute:\n- supply_voltage: values: {model: \"ED, ER PLUS/ER PLUS M, SW-1, ER JR\", value: 12, unit V}, {model: \"PW-II\", value: 9, unit V}.\n\nI could do that at model_level with grouped model labels, or config_level. I'll add it to model_level with grouped names.\n\nBatteries vary by model too:\n- ED, ER PLUS, ER JR: 6 V 3.6 Ah rechargeable\n- ER JR: also 4 × 1.5 V D\n- SW-1: 6 V 3.5 Ah rechargeable\n- PW-II: 6 × 1.5 V AA\n\nCould be battery attributes at model_level with grouped values. I'll include as model_level attribute \"battery\" with string values.\n\nAlso \"check weighing and weighing unstable samples modes\" available only on SW-1*, ER JR, PW-II, ED models (not ER PLUS). Could be a config_level or model_level attribute.\n\nTare config: config_level attribute \"maximum_tare_effect\" with axis \"interval configuration\": single-interval → \"-50% of Max\", dual-interval → \"-Max₁\".\n\n**Matrix tables:** I should preserve the tables as matrix_tables. There are 6 tables (3 single, 3 dual). Third single table is damaged. I'll include them as matrix_tables entries with rows.\n\nTable 1 (single, ED & ER PLUS/M):\ncolumns: model, max_kg, min_g, e_g\nrows: ED: 3/20/1, 6/40/2, 15/100/5, 30/200/10; ER PLUS/ER PLUS M: 6/40/2, 15/100/5, 30/200/10.\n\nTable 2 (single, SW-1 family): 8 rows.\n\nTable 3 (single, ER JR & PW-II): damaged. Rows: ER JR: Max 2,5,10? I'll note description of OCR damage. rows: {\"model\":\"ER JR\",\"max_kg\":2,\"min_g\":20,\"e_g\":1}, {\"model\":\"ER JR\",\"max_kg\":5,...}, ... and PW-II same. Actually with the damaged structure, I'll reconstruct as best I can and note in description.\n\nTable 4 (dual, ED & ER PLUS/M): rows with max as \"1.5 / 3\" strings.\n\nTable 5 (dual, SW-1): 8 rows.\n\nTable 6 (dual, ER JR & PW-II): 6 rows.\n\n**Test reports:** pattern evaluation report P00945, 24 pages. role: type_evaluation_report (pattern evaluation report). id: \"P00945\", pages: 24. Reference No T1128/0107 — that's the certificate reference/approval number, not a test report. Hmm, \"Reference No: T1128/0107\" — NMO internal reference. Could go into certificate.project_number? Possibly. I'll put it as project_number... Actually \"project_number\" might be more like an OIML project. T1128/0107 looks like NMO's file reference. I'll include it as project_number since it's the closest field, or omit. Hmm. Rules say don't fabricate; including it as project_number is a reasonable mapping. I'll include it.\n\n**Revision history:**\n- rev \"\" (original): 27 May 2009, \"Certificate first issued\"\n- rev \"1\": 04 September 2012, \"Load cell and technical data sections added.\"\n- rev \"2\": 12 May 2014, \"Single-interval instruments added. Max tare value added to devices section. Sealing section added.\"\n\n**Recommendation:** id \"R76\", edition 1992, accuracy_classes [\"III\"]. Scheme: B (MAA — the footer says \"OIML R76 MAA Rev. 1 01 April 2011\" and logo shows Mutual Acceptance Arrangement). MAA certificates are Scheme B. I'll set scheme \"B\".\n\n**Footnotes:** none present.\n\n**Certificate page_total:** 4.\n\n**date_issued:** 12 May 2014 → \"2014-05-12\".\n\n**member_state:** \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\" — or just \"United Kingdom\". I'll use the full text.\n\n**oiml_issuer_id:** not present. Omit.\n\n**Module designation:** The instrument comprises a load cell — but as a complete instrument cert, module_designation probably \"Not applicable\". Hmm, R76 complete instrument — I'd say null or \"Not applicable\". I'll use \"Not applicable\" since the load cell is a component of a complete instrument. Actually the load cell is explicitly identified (CAS SW). But the certificate is for the complete weighing instrument, not a module cert. I'll set \"Not applicable\".\n\n**Category:** \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" (R76). Schema example list includes \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\"... I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\".\n\n**Model family:** family_name: \"SW Series\".\n\nNow type_level characteristics:\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"III\", unit_symbol: null}\n- temperature_range: {value: {min: -10, max: 40}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- initial_zero_setting_range: {value: {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol: \"%\"} — \"≤ 20% of Max\". Also reference: \"Max\". Per rule about \"150% of Emax\", add separate reference? I'll add reference strings: initial_zero_setting_reference: \"Max\"? Might be noisy. I'll include value {max: 20} unit \"%\" and a separate characteristic like initial_zero_setting_reference: \"Max\". Hmm, or keep it simple: value: {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol: \"%\", and note reference in the attribute naming? The rules example: `150% of Emax → value: 150, unit: \"%\" and separate characteristic safe_overload_reference: \"Emax\"`. I'll follow that pattern:\n  - initial_zero_setting_range: ≤20%\n  - initial_zero_setting_reference: \"Max\"\n  - semi_automatic_zero_setting_range: ≤4% (reference Max)\n  - zero_tracking_range: ≤4%\n  - tare_range_single_interval: ≤ -50% Max → value {min: -50, max: null}? \"T ≤ -50 % Max\" means tare can subtract up to 50% of Max. I'll record maximum_tare_effect as 50% with config axis.\n  \nConfig-level:\n- attribute: maximum_tare_effect, axis: interval_configuration, values: [{condition: \"single-interval instruments\", value: {min: null, max: -50}?? hmm. \"T ≤ -50% Max\" — the tare balance subtractive, T ≤ -50% Max means the tare indication can go to -50% of Max. I'll record value: 50, unit \"%\", condition \"single-interval\", and for dual: value \"Max₁\" string. Better: value as string \"-50% of Max\"? The structured rule prefers numbers with unit. I'll do: {condition: \"single-interval instruments\", value: -50, unit_symbol: \"%\", reference \"Max\"}? There's no reference field in config values shape... The shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. I'll set value: -50, unit \"%\" — hmm, negative because subtractive. Or \"50% of Max\" as string.\n\nLet me not overthink: config_level:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_tare_effect\",\n  \"axis\": \"interval_configuration\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"single-interval instruments\", \"value\": -50, \"unit_symbol\": \"% of Max\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"dual-interval instruments\", \"value\": \"≤ -Max1\", \"unit_symbol\": null}\n  ]\n}\n```\nHmm, unit \"% of Max\" is clunky. Alternative: value \"-50% of Max\" as string. I think for tare I'll use:\n{\"condition\": \"single-interval instruments\", \"value\": \"-50% of Max\", \"unit_symbol\": null} — keeps fidelity. But rules push numeric separation... The formula rule allows strings for formulas. \"50% of Max\" is like a formula/reference. I'll use value: 50, unit_symbol: \"%\", and mention Max in... no field available. OK compromise: \n- maximum_tare_effect_single: value {min: null, max: 50}, unit \"%\" with the reference encoded in attribute name? No.\n\nI'll do config_level entries:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_tare_effect\",\n  \"axis\": \"interval_configuration\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"single-interval instruments\", \"value\": -50, \"unit_symbol\": \"% of Max\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"dual-interval instruments\", \"value\": \"-Max1 (maximum capacity of lower interval)\", \"unit_symbol\": null}\n  ]\n}\n```\nHmm. Actually \"T ≤ -50% Max\" — I'll simplify: value: -50, unit_symbol: \"%\". And dual: value: \"-Max1\". Good enough, with description fidelity.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe cleaner to put tare at type_level as two attributes:\n- maximum_tare_effect_single_interval: {value: -50, unit: \"%\"} — but \"-50%\" meaning subtractive... The doc: \"T ≤ - 50 % Max (single-interval instruments)\". OK.\n\nI'll go with config_level as designed since schema explicitly wants that for single vs dual variations.\n\nPower supply at type level? It varies by model (12 V vs 9 V) → model_level:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"supply_voltage_dc\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"V\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"ED, ER PLUS/ER PLUS M, SW-1, ER JR\", \"value\": 12},\n    {\"model\": \"PW-II\", \"value\": 9}\n  ]\n}\n```\npower_supply_type: \"DC mains adaptor or battery\".\n\nBatteries → model_level attribute \"battery\":\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"battery\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": null,\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"ED, ER PLUS/ER PLUS M, ER JR\", \"value\": \"6 V 3.6 Ah rechargeable\"},\n    {\"model\": \"ER JR (alternative)\", \"value\": \"4 × 1.5 V D type\"},\n    {\"model\": \"SW-1\", \"value\": \"6 V 3.5 Ah rechargeable\"},\n    {\"model\": \"PW-II\", \"value\": \"6 × 1.5 V AA\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nAlso checkweighing mode availability → model_level:\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"check_weighing_and_unstable_samples_modes\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"ED, SW-1 (all variants), ER JR, PW-II\", \"value\": \"available\"}, {\"model\": \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M\", \"value\": \"not available\"}]}\n```\nHmm, the doc says these modes exist on \"SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II and ED Models only\". I'll record it as a component/device note or config... Maybe better as a type_level characteristic string? It varies by model though. I'll include model_level entry.\n\nNow, the model_level main matrix. Let me define model ids compactly:\n\nSingle-interval (suffix \"_single\"):\nED: ED-3, ED-6, ED-15, ED-30? But model ids should reflect the actual naming. The cert doesn't give capacity-suffixed designations; variants are by Max. I'll use \"ED Max=3kg (single-interval)\" style as in the R60 example (\"Emax=25t\"). \n\nModel ids:\n- \"ED Max=3kg single-interval\"\n- \"ED Max=6kg single-interval\"\n- \"ED Max=15kg single-interval\"\n- \"ED Max=30kg single-interval\"\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M Max=6kg single-interval\"\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M Max=15kg single-interval\"\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M Max=30kg single-interval\"\n- \"SW-1* Max=2kg single-interval\" (SW-1* = SW-1S/1C/1W/1LR/1WR)\n- ... 8 entries\n- \"ER JR Max=2kg single-interval\", \"5kg\", \"10kg\"\n- \"PW-II Max=2kg single-interval\", \"5kg\", \"10kg\"\n\nDual:\n- \"ED Max=1.5/3kg dual-interval\", etc.\n\nThis makes 42 models. The models[] array with attributes per model could embed max/min/e directly in attributes{} per model, and ALSO model_level matrix. The schema: models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. I could put max/min/e in attributes per model — that captures everything without duplicating into model_level. But rule 4 says populate characteristics.model_level. Both allowed. To keep JSON manageable, I'll put the per-model matrix in characteristics.model_level (3 attributes × 42 values each) and give models[] just model_id + label + interval config attribute.\n\nActually, embedding attributes{} per model might be cleaner and less repetitive than model_level arrays... but the rules explicitly show model_level. I'll do model_level with the three attributes. And models[] entries with model_id and attributes {\"interval_configuration\": \"single-interval\"} etc. Hmm, that's duplicative too but fine.\n\nLet me reduce verbosity: models[] entries: {\"model_id\": \"ED Max=3kg (single-interval)\"}. Label maybe \"ED, single-interval, Max 3 kg\". attributes: {\"maximum_capacity\": ...} — no, that's what model_level is for. I'll keep models[] light: model_id + interval_configuration attribute maybe. Actually including attributes{} per model for max/min/e would make model_level redundant. I'll follow the rules: model_level matrix + models[] with ids only (plus maybe interval config attribute).\n\nLet me now write out all values carefully.\n\nSingle-interval tables:\n\nTable 1: ED: (3,20,1), (6,40,2), (15,100,5), (30,200,10). ER PLUS/M: (6,40,2), (15,100,5), (30,200,10).\n\nTable 2: SW-1*: (2,20,1), (3,20,1), (5,40,2), (6,40,2), (10,100,5), (15,100,5), (20,200,10), (30,200,10).\n\nTable 3 (damaged): ER JR: (2,20,1), (5,40,2), (10,100,5); PW-II: (2,20,1), (5,40,2), (10,100,5).\n\nDual tables:\n\nTable 4: ED: (1.5/3, 10, 0.5/1), (3/6, 20, 1/2), (6/15, 40, 2/5), (15/30, 100, 5/10). ER PLUS/M: (3/6, 20, 1/2), (6/15, 40, 2/5), (15/30, 100, 5/10).\n\nTable 5: SW-1*: (1/2, 10, 0.5/1), (1.5/3, 10, 0.5/1), (2.5/5, 20, 1/2), (3/6, 20, 1/2), (4/10, 40, 2/5), (6/15, 40, 2/5), (10/20, 100, 5/10), (15/30, 100, 5/10).\n\nTable 6: ER JR: (3/6, 20, 1/2), (6/15, 40, 2/5), (15/30, 100, 5/10). PW-II: (1/2, 10, 0.5/1), (2.5/5, 20, 1/2), (4/10, 40, 2/5).\n\nFor dual Max and e values in model_level: use {min: 1.5, max: 3} representing Max₁/Max₂? Hmm, \"1.5/3\" is Max₁=1.5, Max₂=3. Using min/max is a reasonable encoding and matches \"ranges become {min,max}\". I'll add unit \"kg\". For e \"0.5/1\" → {min: 0.5, max: 1} unit \"g\".\n\nAlternatively keep as strings \"1.5/3\" — but the rule prefers structured. I'll use min/max objects. Attribute names: maximum_capacity (for Max₁/Max₂ in dual, min=max₁, max=max₂), verification_scale_interval (e). For Min: single value.\n\nNow components:\n```json\n{\n  \"role\": \"load cell\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"SW\"],\n  \"characteristics\": {\"manufacturer\": \"CAS\"}\n}\n```\nWait, characteristics{} for components — shape not strictly defined; I'll put {\"manufacturer\": \"CAS Corporation\"}.\n\nAlso mains adaptor: \"any compatible CE-marked mains adaptor\" — component with alternatives? It says any compatible CE-marked adaptor, so alternatives: null, description. I'll add:\n```json\n{\n  \"role\": \"mains adaptor\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"any compatible CE-marked mains adaptor\"],\n  \"alternatives\": null,\n  \"characteristics\": {\"output_voltage\": ...}\n}\n```\nHmm, the voltage varies by model. Maybe skip mains adaptor as component; keep power supply info in characteristics. I'll include it as a component with description-ish characteristics: {\"voltage_dc\": \"12 V (ED, ER PLUS, SW-1, ER JR) or 9 V (PW-II)\"}. Or just omit. I'll include load cell only, plus maybe batteries as components? No — keep it to load cell. Actually, the certificate \"comprises\" — the instruments comprise load cell CAS SW. I'll include the load cell component. Mains adaptor can be a component with alternatives \"OR\" (any CE-marked). I'll include it briefly.\n\nConstruction features: steel construction, keypad, stainless load receptor, level indicator, operator and customer display. Could go in description or type_level characteristics as strings. I'll add a \"construction\" type_level characteristic? The schema has certified_type.description for free text. I'll fold construction into description.\n\nDevices: initial zero setting, semi-automatic zero, zero tracking, zero indicator, net indicator, semi-automatic subtractive tare, gravity compensation, checkweighing modes. These could be type_level booleans/strings. I'll add key ones with ranges (zero setting ranges). Others into description.\n\nSealing: \"wire-and-seal or tamper-evident sticker\" — description.\n\nDescription: I'll compose: \"Class III, mains or battery-operated, self-indicating, non-automatic weighing instruments designated SW Series and comprising the ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS-M, SW-1, ER JR and PW-II models and their variants. Construction: steel construction, operator's keypad, stainless steel load receptor, level indicator, operator and customer display. Devices: initial zero setting device, semi-automatic zero setting device, zero tracking device, zero indicator, net indicator, semi-automatic subtractive tare balancing device, gravity compensation, check weighing and weighing unstable samples modes (SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II and ED models only). Sealing: access to the load cell and calibration switch prevented by wire-and-seal or tamper-evident sticker.\"\n\nHmm — description should be the free-text description if present; I'll keep it close to source text and not overload.\n\nNow certificate-level:\n```json\n\"certificate\": {\n  \"number\": \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\",\n  \"scheme\": \"B\",\n  \"project_number\": \"T1128/0107\",\n  \"page_total\": 4,\n  \"member_state\": \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2014-05-12\"\n}\n```\nRevision 2 — the certificate number includes revision as separate field; schema has revision_history. Current revision \"2\". I'll include revision in number? The number field: \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\" (revision 2 is separate). I'll keep number without revision and revision_history captures it.\n\nissuing_authority:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"National Measurement Office\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"Paul Dixon\",\n  \"person_title\": \"Director, Product Certification\",\n  \"phone\": \"+44 (0)20 8943 7272\",\n  \"email\": null,\n  \"website\": \"www.bis.gov.uk/nmo\",\n  \"signatory\": \"G Stones\"  // not in schema; omit? Schema doesn't have signatory. Skip.\n}\n```\nperson_responsible: \"Paul Dixon – Director, Product Certification\" → split into person_responsible and person_title. Good.\n\napplicants: CAS Corporation with address lines: [\"19 Ganap-Ri\", \"Gwangjuk-Myoun\", \"Yangju-Si\", \"Gyeonggi-Do 482-841\", \"Republic of Korea\"].\n\nmanufacturers: \n1. CAS Corporation (the applicant) — address same as applicant. The doc says \"Manufacturer: The applicant\" then \"The manufacturer of the certified pattern is the Applicant, or the following companies: ...\" So manufacturers: CAS Corporation (address as applicant), Shanghai CAS Electronics Co., Ltd, CAS Elektronik San. Tic. A.S.\n\ntest_reports:\n```json\n[{\"id\": \"P00945\", \"pages\": 24, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"}]\n```\nDate not given — omit date.\n\nrecommendation:\n```json\n{\"id\": \"R76\", \"edition\": 1992, \"scheme\": \"B\", \"accuracy_classes\": [\"III\"]}\n```\nAmendment: not present. Wait — \"OIML R 76 - Edition 1992(E)\". Edition 1992. But certificate number says R76/1992. OK.\n\nHmm, but there might be R76 1992 edition with the certificate referencing R76/1992-GB1-10.04. Fine.\n\nrevision_history:\n```json\n[\n  {\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": \"2009-05-27\", \"changes\": \"Certificate first issued\"},\n  {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2012-09-04\", \"changes\": \"Load cell and technical data sections added.\"},\n  {\"revision\": \"2\", \"date\": \"2014-05-12\", \"changes\": \"Single-interval instruments added. Max tare value added to devices section. Sealing section added.\"}\n]\n```\nFirst entry revision \"0\"? The doc says \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\" (no rev) — original issue. Schema example: revision \"0\". I'll use \"0\" for the first issue. Hmm, or \"\" — schema says e.g. \"0\". I'll use \"0\".\n\ncertified_type:\n```json\n{\n  \"category\": \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"SW Series\", \"ED\", \"ER PLUS\", \"ER PLUS M\", \"SW-1\", \"SW-1S\", \"SW-1C\", \"SW-1W\", \"SW-1LR\", \"SW-1WR\", \"ER JR\", \"PW-II\"],\n  \"module_designation\": \"Not applicable\",\n  \"description\": \"...\"\n}\n```\n\nmodel_family:\n```json\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"SW Series\",\n  \"models\": [ ... 42 entries ... ]\n}\n```\n\nNow type_level characteristics:\n- accuracy_class: {\"value\": \"III\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}\n- temperature_range: {\"value\": {\"min\": -10, \"max\": 40}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n- initial_zero_setting_range: {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 20}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"} — of Max. Maybe add \"initial_zero_setting_reference\": {\"value\": \"Max\"}.\n- semi_automatic_zero_setting_range: {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 4}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n- zero_tracking_range: {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 4}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n- tare_device_type: \"semi-automatic subtractive tare balancing device\"\n- gravity_compensation: {\"value\": \"provided\"} — eh, maybe skip; description covers.\n- power_supply_type: \"mains adaptor or battery\"\n\nThe zero/tare references \"of Max\" — per rules, add separate reference characteristics? That'd be three extra entries. I'll include references inline via attribute naming? No — rule says use separate characteristic like safe_overload_reference. I'll add:\n- initial_zero_setting_reference: {\"value\": \"Max\"}\n- semi_automatic_zero_setting_reference: {\"value\": \"Max\"}\n- zero_tracking_reference: {\"value\": \"Max\"}\n\nHmm, that's a bit noisy but rule-compliant. Or I could skip references since \"% of Max\" is standard. The rule says \"(Or omit if too noisy.)\" — for the reference characteristic it's optional. I'll include them for completeness but keep names short.\n\nModel_level entries:\n\n1. maximum_capacity (kg): 42 values.\n2. minimum_capacity (g): 42 values.\n3. verification_scale_interval_e (g): 42 values.\n4. supply_voltage_dc (V): grouped.\n5. battery: grouped strings.\n6. checkweighing_mode_availability: grouped.\n\nLet me write the 42 model ids consistently:\n\nSingle-interval:\nS1: \"ED Max=3kg single-interval\" — hmm, example used \"Emax=25t\". I'll use \"ED 3kg (single-interval)\"? Consistency matters. Format: \"<Model> Max=<value>kg <interval>-interval\"? Let me use: \"ED, single-interval, Max 3 kg\". Clearer. Or shorter \"ED-3 (single)\"... The models don't have official capacity-suffixed names, so descriptive ids are fine.\n\nI'll use: \"ED single 3kg\", \"ED single 6kg\", \"ED single 15kg\", \"ED single 30kg\", \"ER PLUS/M single 6kg\", \"ER PLUS/M single 15kg\", \"ER PLUS/M single 30kg\", \"SW-1* single 2kg\", \"SW-1* single 3kg\", \"SW-1* single 5kg\", \"SW-1* single 6kg\", \"SW-1* single 10kg\", \"SW-1* single 15kg\", \"SW-1* single 20kg\", \"SW-1* single 30kg\", \"ER JR single 2kg\", \"ER JR single 5kg\", \"ER JR single 10kg\", \"PW-II single 2kg\", \"PW-II single 5kg\", \"PW-II single 10kg\".\n\nDual: \"ED dual 1.5/3kg\", \"ED dual 3/6kg\", \"ED dual 6/15kg\", \"ED dual 15/30kg\", \"ER PLUS/M dual 3/6kg\", \"ER PLUS/M dual 6/15kg\", \"ER PLUS/M dual 15/30kg\", \"SW-1* dual 1/2kg\", \"SW-1* dual 1.5/3kg\", \"SW-1* dual 2.5/5kg\", \"SW-1* dual 3/6kg\", \"SW-1* dual 4/10kg\", \"SW-1* dual 6/15kg\", \"SW-1* dual 10/20kg\", \"SW-1* dual 15/30kg\", \"ER JR dual 3/6kg\", \"ER JR dual 6/15kg\", \"ER JR dual 15/30kg\", \"PW-II dual 1/2kg\", \"PW-II dual 2.5/5kg\", \"PW-II dual 4/10kg\".\n\nWhere \"SW-1*\" denotes SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR. Maybe use \"SW-1S/1C/1W/1LR/1WR\" — longer but explicit. I'll use \"SW-1S/C/W/LR/WR\" hmm. Let me use \"SW-1x\"? I think explicit: \"SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR\". For model ids that's verbose ×16 entries. I'll use \"SW-1*\" and add a note in models label: label: \"SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, single-interval, Max 2 kg\". Good — label carries the full info.\n\nmodels[] entries: {\"model_id\": \"SW-1* single 2kg\", \"label\": \"SW-1S/SW-1C/SW-1W/SW-1LR/SW-1WR, single-interval, Max 2 kg\", \"attributes\": {\"interval_configuration\": \"single-interval\", \"applicable_submodels\": [\"SW-1S\",\"SW-1C\",\"SW-1W\",\"SW-1LR\",\"SW-1WR\"]}}. Hmm, attributes vary; keep light. Actually schema says models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}} — attributes optional-ish. I'll include interval_configuration attribute for all.\n\nActually, to reduce size, maybe I skip label and just have model_id + attributes. The label is useful for SW-1* expansion. I'll include labels.\n\nLet me now also decide maximum_capacity value encoding for dual: {min: 1.5, max: 3}. I'll add a note in the attribute? No notes field. Fine — min=Max₁, max=Max₂. And verification_scale_interval_e dual: {min: 0.5, max: 1}.\n\nNow matrix_tables: 6 tables. Columns: [\"model\", \"maximum_capacity\", \"minimum_capacity\", \"verification_scale_interval\"]. Units: [null, \"kg\", \"g\", \"g\"]. Rows as objects.\n\nTable 3 damaged — I'll fill rows per my reconstruction and note the OCR damage in description: \"Source table lost its row labels (Max/Min/e) and shows values shifted; reconstructed per dual-interval table correspondence.\" Description: \"OCR-damaged table; row labels missing. Values reconstructed: ER JR and PW-II each with Max 2, 5, 10 kg; Min 20, 40, 100 g; e 1, 2, 5 g; trailing duplicate cells in source treated as artifacts.\"\n\nHmm wait, should I double-check ER JR single again? Given PW-II single = 2/5/10 matches dual upper (2, 5, 10), and the damaged row pattern \"2 5 10 | 2 5 10\" — if the label cell captured the first \"2\", ER JR would be 5, 10, 2 which is nonsense; the sensible reading is ER JR: 2, 5, 10. But dual ER JR upper is 6, 15, 30 — inconsistent. However dual ER JR lower is 3, 6, 15. Neither matches 2, 5, 10. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe I misread dual ER JR: \"3 / 6\", \"6 / 15\", \"15 / 30\" — yes. And single ER JR would presumably be 6, 15, 30 (if single Max = dual Max₂) — but OCR clearly shows 2, 5, 10, 2, 5, 10. Unless the damaged table's ER JR columns actually belong to... hmm, what if the column assignment in the damaged table is: [label, ERJR1, ERJR2, ERJR3, PW1, PW2, PW3] = [2, 5, 10, 2, 5, 10, 2]?? Then ER JR: 5, 10, 2 and PW-II: 5, 10, 2 with trailing 2. Nope.\n\nOr [label, ERJR×3, PW×3] where OCR shifted values left by one from [Max(kg), 5, 10, 20, 2, 5, 10] → \"5 10 20 2 5 10\"? We have \"2 5 10 2 5 10 2\". Not that.\n\nWhat about: original row [Max(kg), 2, 5, 10, 2, 5, 10] and OCR duplicated first value at end? That's my reading. ER JR single: 2, 5, 10. Hmm, but then ER JR single capacities differ from dual. Whatever — could genuinely be that ER JR single-interval models are 2/5/10 kg while ER JR dual are 3/6, 6/15, 15/30. Actually wait — check Min: ER JR single Min 20, 40, 100 with e 1, 2, 5. ER JR dual Min 20, 40, 100 with e₁ 1, 2, 5. Interesting — same Min/e₁. Plausible product line.\n\nHmm, but actually maybe ER JR single Max really is 5, 10, 20: Min 20e → wait Min for 5kg/e1 would be... Min = 20e: 5kg with e=1 → Min 20g ✓ (table shows 20 for first col). If Max were 5, Min 20, e 1 — consistent too. Can't disambiguate. Going with literal OCR: 2, 5, 10.\n\nOK. Also note: the cert says models \"ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS-M, SW-1, ER JR and PW-II and their variants\". So ER PLUS M = ER PLUS-M. I'll use \"ER PLUS-M\" or \"ER PLUS M\"? Tables use \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\". I'll use \"ER PLUS-M\" in type_designations and \"ER PLUS/M\" in model ids... maybe better consistent \"ER PLUS-M\". Let me use \"ER PLUS-M\" everywhere, model ids \"ER PLUS-M single 6kg\" covering both ER PLUS and ER PLUS-M. Label: \"ER PLUS and ER PLUS-M, single-interval, Max 6 kg\".\n\nAlso \"ER JR\" — maybe \"ERJR\"? Keep as \"ER JR\".\n\nSW-1*: I'll write model_id \"SW-1S/C/W/LR/WR single 2kg\"? Hmm, that's cryptic too. I'll use \"SW-1 single 2kg\" with label listing submodels: \"SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR; single-interval; Max 2 kg\". Good.\n\nNow supply_voltage_dc model_level — grouped models. The shape: values: [{model, value, footnote_markers[]}]. Using grouped model strings: {\"model\": \"ED, ER PLUS-M, SW-1, ER JR (single- and dual-interval)\", \"value\": 12, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"} — wait, unit_symbol goes at attribute level in model_level: {\"attribute\", \"unit_symbol\", \"values[{model, value}]\"} per the R137 example. Yes: model_level entry: {\"attribute\": \"supply_voltage_dc\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": 12}, {\"model\": \"PW-II\", \"value\": 9}]}.\n\nBut does SW-1 have mains option? \"Any compatible CE-marked mains adaptor may be used to supply 12 V DC (ED, ER PLUS, SW-1 and ER JR Models)\". Yes SW-1 included.\n\nBattery attribute: values:\n- {\"model\": \"ED, ER PLUS-M, ER JR\", \"value\": \"rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah integrated\"}\n- {\"model\": \"ER JR (alternative)\", \"value\": \"4 × 1.5 V D type integrated\"}\n- {\"model\": \"SW-1\", \"value\": \"rechargeable 6 V 3.5 Ah integrated\"}\n- {\"model\": \"PW-II\", \"value\": \"6 × 1.5 V AA integrated\"}\n\nunit_symbol null.\n\ncheckweighing/unstable-samples modes: \n- {\"model\": \"ED, SW-1, ER JR, PW-II\", \"value\": true}\n- {\"model\": \"ER PLUS-M\", \"value\": false}\nattribute: \"check_weighing_and_weighing_unstable_samples_modes\".\n\nNow config_level:\n1. maximum_tare_effect, axis \"interval_configuration\":\n   - {\"condition\": \"single-interval instruments\", \"value\": -50, \"unit_symbol\": \"% of Max\"} — hmm unit \"% of Max\"... The rule: unit_symbol should be a unit like \"°C\". \"% of Max\" isn't clean. Options: value as string \"-50% of Max\". I'll do value: \"-50% of Max\", unit_symbol: null. It's a formula-ish string, acceptable. And dual: {\"condition\": \"dual-interval instruments\", \"value\": \"-Max1\", \"unit_symbol\": null}. Hmm \"T ≤ - Max₁\". I'll write \"-Max1 (maximum capacity of the lower interval)\". Keep \"-Max1\".\n\nActually, better structured: \n{\"condition\": \"single-interval instruments\", \"value\": {\"min\": -50, \"max\": null}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"} with reference Max implied? The doc literally: \"T ≤ - 50 % Max\". Meaning tare ≤ 50% of Max (subtractive). I'll go with value -50, unit \"%\" — clean and mostly faithful, and note \"of Max\" via a sibling... you know, simplest faithful: value: \"-50% of Max\". I'll use that string form. And \"-Max1\" for dual.\n\n2. Maybe axis for display? No.\n\nSo config_level has one entry (tare). That's fine.\n\nAlso initial zero ≤ 20% Max, semi-auto zero ≤ 4% Max, zero tracking ≤ 4% Max — these are at type_level (apply to all instruments). Good.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nNow let me also consider \"Identification of the certified pattern: SW Series\" — type_designations includes \"SW Series\".\n\nProject number T1128/0107: \"Reference No\". I'll put in certificate.project_number. Hmm — project_number in OIML-CS is like \"P0077\" for the OIML project... Actually for MAA certs, \"project number\" could be the report number P00945. Hmm. T1128/0107 is NMO's reference for the certificate issue. P00945 is the pattern evaluation report. I'll map P00945 to test_reports and T1128/0107 to project_number. Reasonable.\n\nNow build the JSON. Let me carefully write model_level matrices.\n\nmaximum_capacity (kg), single-interval:\n- ED single 3kg: 3; ED single 6kg: 6; ED single 15kg: 15; ED single 30kg: 30\n- ER PLUS-M single 6kg: 6; 15: 15; 30: 30\n- SW-1 single: 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15, 20, 30\n- ER JR single: 2, 5, 10\n- PW-II single: 2, 5, 10\n\ndual (min/max):\n- ED dual 1.5/3: {1.5, 3}; 3/6; 6/15; 15/30\n- ER PLUS-M dual 3/6; 6/15; 15/30\n- SW-1 dual 1/2; 1.5/3; 2.5/5; 3/6; 4/10; 6/15; 10/20; 15/30\n- ER JR dual 3/6; 6/15; 15/30\n- PW-II dual 1/2; 2.5/5; 4/10\n\nminimum_capacity (g):\nsingle: ED: 20, 40, 100, 200; ER PLUS-M: 40, 100, 200; SW-1: 20, 20, 40, 40, 100, 100, 200, 200; ER JR: 20, 40, 100; PW-II: 20, 40, 100\ndual: ED: 10, 20, 40, 100; ER PLUS-M: 20, 40, 100; SW-1: 10, 10, 20, 20, 40, 40, 100, 100; ER JR: 20, 40, 100; PW-II: 10, 20, 40\n\nverification_scale_interval_e (g):\nsingle: ED: 1, 2, 5, 10; ER PLUS-M: 2, 5, 10; SW-1: 1, 1, 2, 2, 5, 5, 10, 10; ER JR: 1, 2, 5; PW-II: 1, 2, 5\ndual: ED: 0.5/1, 1/2, 2/5, 5/10; ER PLUS-M: 1/2, 2/5, 5/10; SW-1: 0.5/1, 0.5/1, 1/2, 1/2, 2/5, 2/5, 5/10, 5/10; ER JR: 1/2, 2/5, 5/10; PW-II: 0.5/1, 1/2, 2/5\n\nModel ids — let me finalize naming: \"ED single-interval 3kg\"? I'll use pattern \"<model> <interval> Max=<v>kg\": e.g. \"ED single-interval Max=3kg\". Slightly long but explicit. Let me use shorter: \"ED-S-3kg\", no... explicitness wins for machine consumption? The example used \"Emax=25t\". I'll go: \"ED Max=3kg (single-interval)\", \"SW-1 Max=2kg (single-interval)\", \"ER PLUS-M Max=3/6kg (dual-interval)\".\n\nHmm OK let me just write it. For SW-1 I'll add label listing the five variants.\n\nLet me now count and write all models[] entries:\n\nSingle-interval (21 entries):\n1. ED Max=3kg (single-interval) — label \"ED, single-interval, Max 3 kg\"\n2. ED Max=6kg (single-interval)\n3. ED Max=15kg (single-interval)\n4. ED Max=30kg (single-interval)\n5. ER PLUS-M Max=6kg (single-interval) — label \"ER PLUS and ER PLUS-M...\"\n6. ER PLUS-M Max=15kg (single-interval)\n7. ER PLUS-M Max=30kg (single-interval)\n8. SW-1 Max=2kg (single-interval) — label \"SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR...\"\n9. SW-1 Max=3kg (single-interval)\n10. SW-1 Max=5kg (single-interval)\n11. SW-1 Max=6kg (single-interval)\n12. SW-1 Max=10kg (single-interval)\n13. SW-1 Max=15kg (single-interval)\n14. SW-1 Max=20kg (single-interval)\n15. SW-1 Max=30kg (single-interval)\n16. ER JR Max=2kg (single-interval)\n17. ER JR Max=5kg (single-interval)\n18. ER JR Max=10kg (single-interval)\n19. PW-II Max=2kg (single-interval)\n20. PW-II Max=5kg (single-interval)\n21. PW-II Max=10kg (single-interval)\n\nDual-interval (21 entries):\n22. ED Max=1.5/3kg (dual-interval)\n23. ED Max=3/6kg (dual-interval)\n24. ED Max=6/15kg (dual-interval)\n25. ED Max=15/30kg (dual-interval)\n26. ER PLUS-M Max=3/6kg (dual-interval)\n27. ER PLUS-M Max=6/15kg (dual-interval)\n28. ER PLUS-M Max=15/30kg (dual-interval)\n29. SW-1 Max=1/2kg (dual-interval)\n30. SW-1 Max=1.5/3kg (dual-interval)\n31. SW-1 Max=2.5/5kg (dual-interval)\n32. SW-1 Max=3/6kg (dual-interval)\n33. SW-1 Max=4/10kg (dual-interval)\n34. SW-1 Max=6/15kg (dual-interval)\n35. SW-1 Max=10/20kg (dual-interval)\n36. SW-1 Max=15/30kg (dual-interval)\n37. ER JR Max=3/6kg (dual-interval)\n38. ER JR Max=6/15kg (dual-interval)\n39. ER JR Max=15/30kg (dual-interval)\n40. PW-II Max=1/2kg (dual-interval)\n41. PW-II Max=2.5/5kg (dual-interval)\n42. PW-II Max=4/10kg (dual-interval)\n\nEach with attributes: {\"interval_configuration\": \"single-interval\"} etc.\n\nGiven size constraints, in models[] I'll include model_id and attributes with interval_configuration, and for SW-1 also submodels list. Labels optional — I'll include for SW-1 only. Actually let me include label for all with concise text? That adds a lot. I'll include labels — they help. Hmm, 42 labels... it's fine, JSON can handle. Actually to keep the output manageable, I'll include label only where it adds info (SW-1 expansion, ER PLUS-M = ER PLUS & ER PLUS-M). For ED/ER JR/PW-II the model_id is self-explanatory. Hmm, but inconsistent. I'll include labels for all — concise.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe skip labels entirely except SW-1 variants where model_id \"SW-1\" is ambiguous. Decide: label null omitted; SW-1 entries get attributes {\"submodels\": [...]}? attributes{} is a free map. I'll put submodels there. Cleaner.\n\nOK now matrix_tables. 6 tables with rows.\n\nTable 1: name \"Single-interval instruments — ED and ER PLUS/ER PLUS M\"\ncolumns: [\"model\", \"maximum_capacity\", \"minimum_capacity\", \"verification_scale_interval\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, \"kg\", \"g\", \"g\"]\nrows:\n{\"model\":\"ED\",\"maximum_capacity\":3,\"minimum_capacity\":20,\"verification_scale_interval\":1}\n{\"model\":\"ED\",\"maximum_capacity\":6,\"minimum_capacity\":40,\"verification_scale_interval\":2}\n{\"model\":\"ED\",\"maximum_capacity\":15,\"minimum_capacity\":100,\"verification_scale_interval\":5}\n{\"model\":\"ED\",\"maximum_capacity\":30,\"minimum_capacity\":200,\"verification_scale_interval\":10}\n{\"model\":\"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\",\"maximum_capacity\":6,\"minimum_capacity\":40,\"verification_scale_interval\":2}\n{\"model\":\"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\",\"maximum_capacity\":15,\"minimum_capacity\":100,\"verification_scale_interval\":5}\n{\"model\":\"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\",\"maximum_capacity\":30,\"minimum_capacity\":200,\"verification_scale_interval\":10}\n\nTable 2: \"Single-interval instruments — SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR\"\nrows: model \"SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR\" with 8 rows.\n\nTable 3: \"Single-interval instruments — ER JR and PW-II\" description about damage. rows:\nER JR: 2/20/1, 5/40/2, 10/100/5; PW-II: 2/20/1, 5/40/2, 10/100/5.\n\nTable 4: \"Dual-interval instruments — ED and ER PLUS/ER PLUS M\"\nrows with max \"1.5 / 3\" → numeric {min,max}? In matrix tables, I'll use objects {min, max} too? Or strings \"1.5/3\". For consistency with model_level, use {\"min\":1.5,\"max\":3}. Hmm in a table row cell, an object is fine JSON. I'll use objects.\n\nTable 5: \"Dual-interval instruments — SW-1...\" 8 rows.\nTable 6: \"Dual-interval instruments — ER JR and PW-II\" 6 rows.\n\nNow type_level characteristics final list:\n- accuracy_class: \"III\"\n- temperature_range: {min:-10, max:40} °C\n- initial_zero_setting_range: {min:null, max:20} \"%\"\n- initial_zero_setting_reference: \"Max\"\n- semi_automatic_zero_setting_range: {min:null, max:4} \"%\"\n- semi_automatic_zero_setting_reference: \"Max\"\n- zero_tracking_range: {min:null, max:4} \"%\"\n- zero_tracking_reference: \"Max\"\n- power_supply_type: \"mains adaptor (CE-marked, any compatible) or battery\"\n- sealing_method: \"wire-and-seal or tamper-evident sticker\"\n\nAlso construction: maybe skip in characteristics (description covers).\n\nStructured value shape: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}. Should I include unit_id always? Rule says leave null unless certain. I'll include \"unit_symbol\" and \"unit_id\": null and footnote_markers empty arrays only where relevant... To keep it clean, include all four keys for type_level. For model_level, shape is {\"attribute\", \"unit_symbol\", \"values\":[{model, value, footnote_markers?}]} — footnote optional; I'll omit when empty. Rule 2 says every characteristic value must be object with value/unit/footnote shape... but the examples in rule 4 show model_level values as plain {\"model\": \"Emax=25t\", \"value\": 25} without unit objects. So model_level: plain values OK with attribute-level unit_symbol. I'll follow the examples.\n\nHmm wait — rule 2 is strict: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". But rule 4's own examples contradict for model_level. The examples are authoritative for model_level format ({\"model\", \"value\"}). I'll add footnote_markers only when present. For type_level, use full shape with value/unit_symbol/unit_id.\n\nLet me decide: type_level entries: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}. I'll include unit_id null and empty footnote arrays. Fine.\n\ndescription for certified_type — compose faithful text:\n\n\"Class III, mains or battery-operated, self-indicating, non-automatic weighing instruments designated SW Series and comprising the ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS-M, SW-1, ER JR and PW-II models and their variants, in single-interval and dual-interval versions. Construction: steel construction, operator's keypad, stainless steel load receptor, level indicator, operator and customer display. Devices: initial zero setting device (≤ 20% of Max), semi-automatic zero setting device (≤ 4% of Max), zero tracking device (≤ 4% of Max), zero indicator, net indicator, semi-automatic subtractive tare balancing device, gravity compensation, and check weighing and weighing unstable samples modes (SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II and ED models only). Sealing: access to the load cell and calibration switch is prevented by sealing the enclosure using a wire-and-seal or tamper-evident sticker. Load cell: CAS load cell, model SW. Power supply: any compatible CE-marked mains adaptor supplying 12 V DC (ED, ER PLUS, SW-1 and ER JR models) or 9 V DC (PW-II model), or integrated batteries.\"\n\nGood.\n\nComponents:\n1. {\"role\": \"load cell\", \"type_designations\": [\"CAS SW\"], \"alternatives\": null, \"characteristics\": {\"manufacturer\": \"CAS Corporation\"}}\n2. {\"role\": \"mains adaptor\", \"type_designations\": [\"any compatible CE-marked mains adaptor\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\", \"characteristics\": {\"output_voltage\": \"12 V DC (ED, ER PLUS, SW-1, ER JR) or 9 V DC (PW-II)\"}}\n\nHmm, characteristics values as objects? Components characteristics{} shape unspecified. Keep simple strings. Or skip mains adaptor component. I'll include both.\n\nTest report: {\"id\": \"P00945\", \"pages\": 24, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"} — date omitted.\n\nNow scheme: Is this scheme A or B? \"OIML – MUTUAL ACCEPTANCE ARRANGEMENT\" logo + footer \"OIML R76 MAA Rev. 1 01 April 2011\" strongly indicates MAA = scheme B. Set \"B\".\n\nrecommendation.scheme: \"B\" too.\n\nedition: 1992.\n\nNow, the \"certificate.number\": \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\". Include \"Revision 2\"? The revision_history covers it. Fine.\n\nLet me also double check applicant address: \"19 Ganap-Ri, Gwangjuk-Myoun, Yangju-Si, Gyeonggi-Do 482-841, Republic of Korea\". Yes.\n\nShanghai CAS: \"Maixinroad 448, Xinqiaozhen, Songjiangqu, Shanghai, China\".\n\nCAS Elektronik: \"Yukari Dudulu, Bostanci Cad. Mevdudi Sokak No: 34, Umraniye-Istanbul, Turkey\".\n\nManufacturers array: first entry \"The applicant\" → CAS Corporation with same address. Include note? I'll just list CAS Corporation.\n\nNow write the final JSON. Be careful with valid JSON, no trailing commas, × character fine (UTF-8). Use \"°C\" fine.\n\nFor dual capacity {min, max} in model_level values — value: {\"min\": 1.5, \"max\": 3}.\n\nLet me draft model_level entries:\n\n```json\n\"model_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_capacity\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"model\": \"ED Max=3kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 3},\n      {\"model\": \"ED Max=6kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 6},\n      {\"model\": \"ED Max=15kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 15},\n      {\"model\": \"ED Max=30kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 30},\n      {\"model\": \"ER PLUS-M Max=6kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 6},\n      {\"model\": \"ER PLUS-M Max=15kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 15},\n      {\"model\": \"ER PLUS-M Max=30kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 30},\n      {\"model\": \"SW-1 Max=2kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 2},\n      {\"model\": \"SW-1 Max=3kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 3},\n      {\"model\": \"SW-1 Max=5kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 5},\n      {\"model\": \"SW-1 Max=6kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 6},\n      {\"model\": \"SW-1 Max=10kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 10},\n      {\"model\": \"SW-1 Max=15kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 15},\n      {\"model\": \"SW-1 Max=20kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 20},\n      {\"model\": \"SW-1 Max=30kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 30},\n      {\"model\": \"ER JR Max=2kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 2},\n      {\"model\": \"ER JR Max=5kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 5},\n      {\"model\": \"ER JR Max=10kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 10},\n      {\"model\": \"PW-II Max=2kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 2},\n      {\"model\": \"PW-II Max=5kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 5},\n      {\"model\": \"PW-II Max=10kg (single-interval)\", \"value\": 10},\n      {\"model\": \"ED Max=1.5/3kg (dual-interval)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 1.5, \"max\": 3}},\n      {\"model\": \"ED Max=3/6kg (dual-interval)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 3, \"max\": 6}},\n      {\"model\":